  "description": "issue #688 (terminal slice): item 1 of the normative same-tick delivery list, `pending when events in registration order` (spec/interaction-events.md:84-89), proven at a genuine shared drain point. Two handlers for the same named event `go` are registered, then ONE `go` is delivered at tick 1 during a `wait 1`, so both are pending together and fire in registration order -- printing 1 then 2. The existing `when-registration-order` fixture covers only `\"start\"`, which a batch run is ALREADY delivering, so there each handler fires immediately upon its own registration and the two are never pending simultaneously; its own description records this (`handler a completes entirely before handler b is registered`). That sequencing follows from statement order alone and would hold even in an implementation whose pending-event queue drained backwards, so it cannot discriminate item 1 -- this fixture can, because a reversed drain prints 2 then 1. The handler-start `instruction` spans (seq 5 = the line-1 `when` head, seq 8 = the line-2 head, spec/interaction-events.md:101-102) pin the same order a second, independent way. `wait` emits its `primitive` after the pause completes (seq 11), and the delivered event word never appears in any payload -- the stream stays headless.",
